+### Write-path knobs for A/B tests
+
+Environment variables of the Core service (a systemd drop-in with `Environment=`). Each one restores an older behaviour or switches a measured option on, so a change can be compared on the same rig. Details and the measurements behind them: the Apertar knowledge base, `working/dng-chain`.
+
+| Variable | Default | What it changes |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `APERTAR_DNG_PAYLOAD_COPY=1` | off | Copies every frame's rows into the writer's scratch buffer before writing, even when the copy changes nothing (the behaviour before the direct write of 16-bit and 8-bit payloads). |
+| `APERTAR_DNG_WRITE_ROWS=N` | 64 | Rows per `write()` when the payload goes through the scratch buffer (packed 10/12-bit, PiSP-compressed and CineMate Log frames). |
+| `APERTAR_RAW_POOL_WARM=0` | on | Restores the old raw-copy pool: no copies allocated before a take starts, and at most workers + 2 kept while it runs. |
+| `APERTAR_DNG_WRITEBACK=0` | on | No `sync_file_range` per written DNG (the kernel's own writeback only). |
+| `APERTAR_MEDIA_SYNC_MS=0` | 1000 | No once-a-second `fsync` of the finished DNGs and the clip folder during a take; the stop-time `syncfs` stays. |
+| `APERTAR_RT=1` | off | The start script runs the whole Core process under `SCHED_FIFO` priority 70 on cores 1 to 3 with the best-effort I/O class (`APERTAR_RT_PRIORITY`, `APERTAR_RT_CPUS` adjust it), as cinemate runs cinepi-raw. An A/B instrument, not a recommendation: equal-priority FIFO threads never preempt each other, so the capture thread can wait behind FIFO writers. Needs `LimitRTPRIO` on the unit: install `config/apertar-core-capture-priority.conf` as a drop-in. |
+| `APERTAR_MEDIA_TUNE=1` | off | Kernel-side knobs for the recording drive through the `apertar-media-tune` root helper (dirty-page cushion, scheduler, queue depth). |
+
 ## For Developers
 
 ApertarCore is the source of truth for camera and capture state. Native UI commands, WebUI requests and hardware actions reach the same controller and busy-state checks.
diff --git a/config/apertar-core-capture-priority.conf b/config/apertar-core-capture-priority.conf
index c0df914..01cc640 100644
--- a/config/apertar-core-capture-priority.conf
+++ b/config/apertar-core-capture-priority.conf
@@ -1,3 +1,6 @@
 [Service]
 # Permit only a bounded, non-real-time boost for the camera callback thread.
 LimitNICE=-5
+# Needed only with APERTAR_RT=1 in the unit's environment: the start script then runs Core under
+# SCHED_FIFO 70 (chrt), as cinemate runs cinepi-raw. Without APERTAR_RT=1 the limit is unused.
+LimitRTPRIO=80
diff --git a/scripts/start-apertar-core.sh b/scripts/start-apertar-core.sh
index 313f784..954a5d7 100755
--- a/scripts/start-apertar-core.sh
+++ b/scripts/start-apertar-core.sh
@@ -17,5 +17,28 @@ if [[ -n "${APERTAR_TUNING_FILE:-}" ]]; then
     export LIBCAMERA_RPI_TUNING_FILE="$APERTAR_TUNING_FILE"
 fi
 
+# APERTAR_RT=1: run Core under SCHED_FIFO (APERTAR_RT_PRIORITY, default 70) on the cores in
+# APERTAR_RT_CPUS (default 1-3, empty = no pinning) with the best-effort I/O class, the way
+# cinemate starts cinepi-raw (src/module/cinepi_multi.py run()). The unit needs LimitRTPRIO
+# (config/apertar-core-capture-priority.conf). When chrt is refused Core starts as before.
+# Off by default: the A/B in the kb (working/dng-chain) decides whether it stays.
+PREFIX=()
+if [[ "${APERTAR_RT:-0}" == "1" ]]; then
+    RT_PRIORITY="${APERTAR_RT_PRIORITY:-70}"
+    RT_CPUS="${APERTAR_RT_CPUS-1-3}"
+    if command -v chrt >/dev/null 2>&1 && chrt -f "$RT_PRIORITY" /bin/true 2>/dev/null; then
+        PREFIX+=(chrt -f "$RT_PRIORITY")
+        if command -v ionice >/dev/null 2>&1; then
+            PREFIX+=(ionice -c2 -n0)
+        fi
+        if [[ -n "$RT_CPUS" ]] && command -v taskset >/dev/null 2>&1; then
+            PREFIX+=(taskset -c "$RT_CPUS")
+        fi
+        echo "ApertarCore start: real-time scheduling on: ${PREFIX[*]}" >&2
+    else
+        echo "ApertarCore start: APERTAR_RT=1 but chrt -f $RT_PRIORITY is refused (is LimitRTPRIO set on the unit?); starting normally" >&2
+    fi
+fi
+
 mkdir -p "$(dirname "$SOCKET_PATH")"
-exec "$CORE_BIN" --socket "$SOCKET_PATH" --media "$MEDIA_ROOT"
+exec ${PREFIX[@]+"${PREFIX[@]}"} "$CORE_BIN" --socket "$SOCKET_PATH" --media "$MEDIA_ROOT"
